## Statement

For a complex Lie superalgebra $\mathfrak g$, every odd $x\in\mathfrak g_{\bar1}$ satisfies $[x,[x,x]]=0$.

## Facts & Assumptions

**Given:** A complex Lie superalgebra $\mathfrak g$ and an odd element $x\in\mathfrak g_{\bar1}$.

[F1] [[def-lie-superalgebra-over-complex-numbers]]: for homogeneous elements, $[x,[y,z]]=\left\lbrack [x,y],z\right\rbrack+(-1)^{|x||y|}[y,[x,z]]$, and $[a,b]=-(-1)^{|a||b|}[b,a]$.

## Proof




1.1 Put $A=[x,[x,x]]$. The signed Jacobi identity in [F1], with $y=z=x$, gives $A=\left\lbrack [x,x],x\right\rbrack-A$, because $(-1)^{|x||x|}=-1$. [given, F1, algebra]


2.1 The element $[x,x]$ is even, so graded skew symmetry in [F1] gives $\left\lbrack [x,x],x\right\rbrack=-[x,[x,x]]=-A$. Substituting into step 1.1 yields $A=-2A$, hence $3A=0$; since the scalar field is $\mathbb C$, $A=0$. [given, F1, step 1.1, algebra] ∎
